{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148523",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
